-- ============================================================
-- Quiz coverage for cloud/aws-lambda, cloud/aws-eks,
-- cloud/cloudformation, cloud/route53 -- overview, fundamentals,
-- intermediate, advanced tabs (the four tabs quizzed consistently
-- across batches 1-2), grounded in this batch's corrected content.
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO quiz_questions (academy_slug, technology_slug, section_slug, question, options, correct_index, explanation, difficulty) VALUES

-- ---------- cloud/aws-lambda/overview ----------
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'overview', 'What actually happens during a Lambda "cold start"?', '["Lambda throttles the function to save cost", "AWS has to create a new execution environment (container), download your code, initialize the runtime, and run your code outside the handler -- before your handler function can run", "The function is paused until you manually restart it", "Cold start only happens on the very first deploy, never again"]', 1, 'A cold start happens whenever no warm container is available to reuse -- after idle periods, on scale-up beyond currently-warm capacity, or on a fresh deploy. Code outside the handler (client initialization, etc.) runs once per cold start, not once per invocation.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'overview', 'In the SQS partial-batch-failure pattern (returning batchItemFailures), what happens to messages NOT included in that list?', '["They are deleted from the queue whether they succeeded or not", "They are treated as successfully processed and removed from the queue; only the itemIdentifiers actually listed are retried", "The entire batch is retried regardless of what is returned", "SQS ignores the return value entirely"]', 1, 'Without reporting batchItemFailures at all, a single failed message in a batch causes Lambda to retry the ENTIRE batch, including messages that already succeeded. Returning the specific failed itemIdentifiers scopes the retry to just those.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'overview', 'Why does the SAM template initialize boto3 clients (like the DynamoDB resource) outside the handler function rather than inside it?', '["boto3 clients cannot be created inside a function", "Code outside the handler runs once per container and is reused across warm invocations, avoiding the cost of recreating the client (and its connection) on every single invocation", "AWS requires all imports to be at module level", "It has no effect on performance either way"]', 1, 'This is the standard Lambda optimization: anything expensive to set up (SDK clients, DB connections) should be created once at module scope so warm invocations reuse it, instead of paying that setup cost on every single request.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'overview', 'Why has this batch corrected the claim that attaching a Lambda function to a VPC always adds meaningful cold-start latency?', '["VPC-attached Lambdas have never had any cold-start penalty at any point", "AWS re-architected Lambda VPC networking in 2019 (shared Hyperplane ENIs), which removed most of the historical ENI-creation cold-start penalty -- it is no longer the dominant cold-start factor it once was, though older material still frames it that way", "VPC-attached functions are now always faster than non-VPC functions", "Lambda no longer supports VPC configuration at all"]', 1, 'Before 2019, attaching Lambda to a VPC meant creating a new ENI per concurrent execution environment, which was slow. The Hyperplane ENI change lets functions in the same subnet/security-group combination share pre-created ENIs, largely eliminating that specific penalty.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'overview', 'Roughly how does Lambda pricing scale with the memory setting for a CPU-bound function, given that CPU allocation is proportional to memory?', '["Memory has no effect on cost since GB-second rate is fixed","Increasing memory raises the GB-second rate paid, but can also make the function finish faster (more CPU) -- so total cost for a CPU-bound function is not simply \"lower memory = cheaper\" and should be measured, not assumed","Lower memory always minimizes total cost for every workload","Memory only affects /tmp storage, not billing"]', 1, 'Because CPU scales with memory below the ~1,769MB (1 vCPU) point, a CPU-bound function at 128MB can take proportionally much longer to run, potentially costing MORE in total GB-seconds than the same function at a higher memory setting that finishes faster. Tools like AWS Lambda Power Tuning exist specifically to measure this instead of guessing.', 'advanced'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-lambda/fundamentals ----------
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'fundamentals', 'In the Terraform aws_lambda_function resource, what is the purpose of source_code_hash?', '["It encrypts the deployment package", "It tells Terraform whether the deployed code has actually changed, so it knows whether an update is needed on the next apply", "It sets the function''s execution timeout", "It is required only for container-image-based functions"]', 1, 'Without source_code_hash, Terraform has no reliable way to detect that the zip contents changed if the filename stayed the same -- the hash of the archive content is what triggers a redeploy on the next apply.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'fundamentals', 'Why does the IAM role for a Lambda function need a trust policy with Principal: {Service: "lambda.amazonaws.com"}?', '["It is optional -- Lambda can assume any role automatically", "This trust policy is what actually allows the Lambda service itself to assume that IAM role at invocation time; without it, the role cannot be attached to a function", "It grants the function access to invoke other Lambda functions", "It is only needed for functions triggered by API Gateway"]', 1, 'An IAM role''s trust policy (AssumeRolePolicyDocument) controls WHO/WHAT can assume it. For a Lambda execution role, that has to explicitly include the Lambda service principal, or Lambda cannot use the role to obtain the permissions it grants.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'fundamentals', 'What is Lambda Layers used for?', '["Adding more memory to a function beyond the 10,240MB limit", "Sharing common code/dependencies across multiple functions as a separate, reusable ZIP package, so you don''t have to bundle the same dependency into every function''s deployment package", "Automatically scaling concurrent executions", "Enabling VPC access for a function"]', 1, 'A Layer is deployed and versioned separately from the function code itself -- update the layer once, and every function referencing that layer version benefits, rather than re-bundling shared dependencies into each function individually.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'fundamentals', 'When an EventBridge (formerly CloudWatch Events) schedule expression like rate(5 minutes) triggers a Lambda function, is that invocation synchronous or asynchronous?', '["Synchronous -- EventBridge waits for the function to finish and return a value", "Asynchronous -- EventBridge hands off the event and does not wait for or use a return value from the function", "It depends on the function''s configured timeout", "Neither -- scheduled invocations use the Event Source Mapping polling model"]', 1, 'Scheduled EventBridge triggers, like S3 and SNS triggers, invoke Lambda asynchronously: the event is queued for processing and the caller does not wait on the function''s result.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'fundamentals', 'In the fundamentals VPC config example, why would a Lambda function need vpc_config with private_subnet_ids and a security group at all?', '["Every Lambda function requires VPC config to run", "Only to reach resources that live inside a VPC and are not publicly reachable, such as an RDS database in a private subnet", "To reduce cold start time", "To increase the function''s maximum execution timeout"]', 1, 'A Lambda function with no vpc_config already has full internet access via the AWS-managed network and can reach public AWS service endpoints. VPC config is needed specifically to reach private-subnet resources like an RDS instance that has no public endpoint.', 'beginner'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-lambda/intermediate ----------
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'intermediate', 'In the SNS fan-out pattern, why publish to a single SNS topic rather than invoking each Lambda function directly?', '["SNS is required for any Lambda-to-Lambda communication", "Multiple independent Lambda functions can subscribe to the same topic (with different filter policies), so the publisher does not need to know or maintain a list of every downstream consumer", "SNS is the only way to trigger Lambda from S3", "Publishing to SNS is always faster than a direct invocation"]', 1, 'Fan-out via SNS decouples the publisher from consumers: adding a new downstream processor (e.g. a virus scanner) just means subscribing it to the existing topic, with no change needed to the code that publishes the upload event.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'intermediate', 'What does an SNS subscription FilterPolicy do in the SAM template example?', '["It encrypts messages before delivery", "It restricts which published messages actually get delivered to that specific subscriber, based on message attributes -- e.g. only image/jpeg or image/png uploads reach the ImageProcessor function", "It sets the maximum retry count for failed deliveries", "It converts the message format from JSON to XML"]', 1, 'Without a filter policy, every subscriber to a topic receives every message published to it. A FilterPolicy lets each subscriber declare which subset of messages (by attribute) it actually wants delivered.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'intermediate', 'What is the correct way to apply AWS Lambda Powertools'' tracing to a specific function, rather than using it as a Python context manager?', '["with tracer.capture_method: ...", "@tracer.capture_method applied as a decorator directly on the function to be traced", "tracer.start_trace() / tracer.end_trace() called manually", "Tracing cannot be scoped to individual functions"]', 1, 'capture_method in AWS Lambda Powertools is a decorator, not a context manager -- it is meant to be applied directly above a function definition (@tracer.capture_method), not used with a "with" statement.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'intermediate', 'What does AWS Lambda Power Tuning actually measure?', '["Network latency between AWS regions", "Cost and execution duration for a function running at different memory settings, to find the empirically optimal memory/cost tradeoff instead of guessing", "The maximum concurrency a function can sustain", "Code coverage of unit tests"]', 1, 'Rather than assuming lower memory is always cheaper (it isn''t, for CPU-bound workloads), Power Tuning actually runs the function at several memory sizes and reports real cost/duration data so the choice is measured, not guessed.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'intermediate', 'What is the purpose of a Lambda Extension, as described for the DataDog/OpenTelemetry example?', '["To increase a function''s maximum timeout beyond 15 minutes", "To run supporting processes (telemetry, secrets fetching, monitoring agents) alongside the function''s main execution environment, without modifying the function''s own code", "To replace the function''s runtime entirely", "To automatically retry failed invocations"]', 1, 'Extensions run as a companion process inside the same execution environment as the function, commonly used for observability/APM agents and secrets retrieval -- they augment the environment without requiring changes to the handler logic itself.', 'advanced'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-lambda/advanced ----------
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'advanced', 'What problem does Lambda SnapStart solve for Java functions specifically?', '["It reduces the maximum memory a Java function can use", "It dramatically reduces Java cold starts by initializing the JVM and application code once, taking a snapshot, and restoring execution environments from that snapshot instead of re-running full JVM startup each time", "It removes the need for an IAM execution role", "It converts Java bytecode to a faster native binary automatically"]', 1, 'Java historically had the worst cold starts among common Lambda runtimes (JVM startup + class loading). SnapStart persists a post-initialization snapshot so new execution environments restore from it rather than repeating the full JVM boot and static initialization every time.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'advanced', 'In the SnapStart Terraform example, why must publish = true and a snap_start block both be set?', '["publish = true is unrelated to SnapStart and only affects versioning","SnapStart requires the function to be invoked via a published version (not $LATEST) -- apply_on = \"PublishedVersions\" only takes effect once a version actually exists to snapshot","snap_start alone is sufficient; publish is optional","Both settings are deprecated in favor of Provisioned Concurrency"]', 1, 'SnapStart operates on published versions, not the mutable $LATEST alias -- a version has to exist for AWS to create and restore snapshots from, which is why publish = true is required alongside the snap_start configuration.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'advanced', 'In the Step Functions state machine JSON, what does the Retry block on the ValidateOrder state actually control?', '["Whether the entire state machine execution is retried from the start", "Automatic re-invocation of that specific Lambda task on the listed error types, using the given interval/backoff/max-attempts, before failing over to any Catch handler", "The number of times a human must manually approve the step", "How many parallel branches the state can spawn"]', 1, 'Retry is scoped to the individual state -- it lets Step Functions re-invoke just that failing task (with exponential backoff via BackoffRate) a bounded number of times before giving up and falling through to a Catch handler, if one is defined.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'advanced', 'What does the AWS CLI shorthand --routing-config AdditionalVersionWeights={4=0.1} do when updating a Lambda alias?', '["Routes 4% of traffic permanently to version 0.1", "Sends approximately 10% of invocations through this alias to function version 4, with the remainder going to the alias''s primary target version -- the mechanism behind Lambda traffic-shifting/canary deploys", "Sets the function''s reserved concurrency to 4", "Deletes version 4 after 0.1 seconds"]', 1, 'AdditionalVersionWeights lets an alias split invocations between its primary target version and one or more additional versions by weight -- here, 10% of traffic goes to version 4, letting you canary-test a new version before shifting it to 100%.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-lambda', 'advanced', 'Why does the cheatsheet''s CloudWatch get-metric-statistics example query the Errors metric with Statistic=Sum rather than Average?', '["Sum and Average always return the same value for error counts", "Errors is a count-type metric -- summing it over the period gives the total number of failed invocations in that window, whereas averaging a count metric produces a less directly useful number", "Average is not a valid statistic for any Lambda metric", "Sum only works with Duration, never with Errors"]', 1, 'For count-based metrics like Errors, Sum answers "how many failures happened in this period," which is the actionable number for alerting/dashboards -- Average would instead answer a less intuitive question about the metric''s per-datapoint mean.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-eks/overview ----------
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'overview', 'In EKS, what specifically does AWS manage versus what the customer manages?', '["AWS manages everything, including worker nodes and application pods", "AWS manages the control plane (API server, etcd, scheduler) across multiple AZs; the customer manages worker nodes (EC2 or Fargate) and everything deployed onto the cluster", "The customer manages the control plane; AWS only provides worker node AMIs", "EKS has no control plane -- it is entirely customer-managed"]', 1, 'This division is the core value proposition of a managed Kubernetes service: the operationally painful part (etcd HA, API server availability, control-plane patching) is AWS''s responsibility, while the customer keeps full control over node sizing, add-ons, and workloads.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'overview', 'What problem does IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) solve compared to giving AWS permissions via the EC2 instance profile on worker nodes?', '["IRSA is required before any pod can start on EKS", "With instance profiles, every pod on a node shares the same AWS permissions as the node itself; IRSA scopes AWS permissions to an individual Kubernetes ServiceAccount instead, so a compromised pod''s blast radius is limited to just that pod''s role", "IRSA removes the need for Kubernetes RBAC entirely", "IRSA only works with Fargate, not EC2 worker nodes"]', 1, 'Instance-profile-based access means any pod scheduled on a node inherits whatever AWS permissions that node''s role has -- a real security risk in multi-tenant clusters. IRSA uses OIDC federation so each ServiceAccount gets its own scoped IAM role via temporary STS credentials.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'overview', 'What is the tradeoff of running EKS worker nodes on Fargate instead of managed EC2 node groups?', '["Fargate is always cheaper for every workload type", "Fargate eliminates node management (each pod runs on its own micro-VM), but it cannot run DaemonSets, privileged pods, or GPU workloads -- so it fits some workload types and not others", "Fargate requires manually patching the underlying host OS", "Fargate only supports stateful workloads, not stateless ones"]', 1, 'Fargate trades operational simplicity (no AMIs, no node patching, no capacity planning) for real constraints: DaemonSets need a real node to run one-per-node, privileged/GPU workloads are unsupported, making it a fit for stateless/dev/batch workloads rather than a universal replacement for managed node groups.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'overview', 'Why is a Kubernetes ServiceAccount''s namespace part of what determines whether it can assume a given IRSA IAM role?', '["Namespaces have no bearing on IRSA -- only the ServiceAccount name matters", "The IAM trust policy''s condition typically matches on the full system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name> subject string, so a ServiceAccount with the same NAME in a different namespace produces a different subject and does not match", "IRSA ignores namespaces entirely and grants access cluster-wide", "Namespace only matters for network policies, not IAM"]', 1, 'The OIDC token''s "sub" claim encodes both namespace and ServiceAccount name (system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name>). A trust policy condition scoped to system:serviceaccount:default:my-app will NOT match a same-named ServiceAccount in a different namespace like staging.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'overview', 'Per this batch''s correction, is the $0.10/hour EKS control-plane cost something a learner should treat as fixed and current?', '["Yes, AWS pricing for managed services never changes once published", "No -- it should be flagged and verified against the current EKS pricing page, since exact pricing is a detail that can change over time even though the general fact (there IS a per-cluster control-plane charge, it is not free) is stable", "No -- EKS control plane has actually always been free", "Pricing verification is unnecessary since AWS guarantees price stability by contract"]', 1, 'The accuracy-confidence rule applied here: the architectural fact (a per-cluster control-plane charge exists, distinct from EC2/Fargate costs) is safe to state directly, but the exact dollar figure is pricing-specific and should be verified against AWS''s current pricing page rather than assumed static.', 'beginner'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-eks/fundamentals ----------
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'fundamentals', 'In the Terraform eks_managed_node_groups block, what does setting capacity_type = "SPOT" on the spot node group actually change?', '["It disables autoscaling for that node group entirely", "Nodes in that group are provisioned as EC2 Spot Instances rather than On-Demand, trading a risk of interruption for a substantially lower price", "It forces all pods to be rescheduled every hour", "It changes the node group''s Kubernetes version independently of the cluster"]', 1, 'Spot capacity_type uses EC2 Spot pricing/availability instead of On-Demand -- appropriate for interruption-tolerant workloads (the taints on the spot group in the example are there specifically so nothing schedules there unless it explicitly tolerates the spot taint).', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'fundamentals', 'Why does the IRSA trust policy example use a Condition block matching ${CLUSTER_OIDC}:sub rather than just trusting the OIDC provider unconditionally?', '["Conditions are required by IAM syntax even if they do nothing useful", "Without the sub condition, ANY ServiceAccount in ANY namespace of that cluster could assume the role -- the condition scopes trust to one specific ServiceAccount", "The condition only affects logging, not actual access", "OIDC providers cannot be trusted without a condition due to an AWS API limitation"]', 1, 'The Federated principal alone establishes trust in the OIDC provider generally; the StringEquals condition on the sub claim is what narrows that trust down to one specific namespace/ServiceAccount combination, which is the entire point of IRSA''s security model.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'fundamentals', 'What is the practical difference between an EKS "add-on" (like the VPC CNI or CoreDNS, managed via aws eks update-addon) and a tool like the AWS Load Balancer Controller?', '["There is no difference -- both are installed identically", "EKS add-ons have their lifecycle (install/update/patch) managed by AWS/EKS directly; tools like the AWS Load Balancer Controller are installed and upgraded separately (e.g. via Helm) and are not EKS-managed add-ons", "The AWS Load Balancer Controller is deprecated in favor of add-ons", "Add-ons can only be used with Fargate, never with EC2 node groups"]', 1, 'A defined, evolving set of components (vpc-cni, coredns, kube-proxy, EBS/EFS CSI drivers, etc.) are offered as EKS-managed add-ons with their own versioning and update API. Other common cluster tooling, like the AWS Load Balancer Controller or External DNS, is not (as of this writing) offered that way and is installed independently.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'fundamentals', 'Why does the Cluster Autoscaler deployment need a ServiceAccount annotated with an IAM role ARN (via IRSA)?', '["Cluster Autoscaler does not require any AWS permissions", "Cluster Autoscaler needs to call AWS APIs (to resize Auto Scaling Groups) on the cluster''s behalf, and IRSA is how it gets scoped AWS credentials without static access keys stored in the cluster", "The annotation only affects Kubernetes RBAC, not AWS access", "IRSA is only needed for application pods, never for cluster infrastructure components"]', 1, 'Cluster Autoscaler is itself just a pod running in the cluster -- to call aws:autoscaling APIs and adjust ASG desired capacity, it needs real AWS credentials, and IRSA is the standard way to grant that without embedding long-lived keys.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'fundamentals', 'What does the EBS CSI driver add-on provide that isn''t available without it?', '["Node-level networking (pod IP assignment)", "Dynamic provisioning of EBS volumes as Kubernetes PersistentVolumes, so a PVC can automatically create a real EBS volume instead of requiring one to be pre-created manually", "DNS resolution for services inside the cluster", "Load balancer creation for Ingress resources"]', 1, 'The EBS CSI driver implements the Container Storage Interface for EBS, letting StorageClasses dynamically provision EBS volumes on demand as PersistentVolumeClaims are created, rather than requiring volumes to exist beforehand.', 'beginner'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-eks/intermediate ----------
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'intermediate', 'How does Karpenter''s node-provisioning approach differ fundamentally from Cluster Autoscaler''s?', '["Karpenter can only provision Spot instances, never On-Demand", "Karpenter provisions nodes directly, choosing an appropriate instance type/size for pending pods on demand, rather than scaling a pre-defined, fixed-shape node group like Cluster Autoscaler does", "Cluster Autoscaler is faster because it pre-warms nodes", "Karpenter requires manually specifying the exact node for every pod"]', 1, 'Cluster Autoscaler scales the size of existing, pre-defined node groups. Karpenter instead evaluates pending pods'' actual resource requests and provisions right-sized nodes directly from a flexible set of allowed instance types -- generally resulting in faster, better-fitted scaling.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'intermediate', 'Per this batch''s flagged correction, why should the Karpenter NodePool/EC2NodeClass apiVersion (karpenter.sh/v1beta1) not be trusted as permanently correct?', '["Karpenter CRDs never change their apiVersion once released", "Karpenter''s CRD API group has moved through multiple versions over its release history (v1alpha5 -> v1beta1 -> v1 as of Karpenter 1.0), so the exact apiVersion to use depends on which Karpenter version is actually being installed", "apiVersion is purely cosmetic and has no functional effect", "Only EC2NodeClass has a version-specific apiVersion; NodePool does not"]', 1, 'This is exactly the kind of version-specific detail the accuracy-confidence rule requires flagging rather than silently asserting -- a learner following this example must check which Karpenter version they''re actually installing and match its current CRD apiVersion.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'intermediate', 'What is the core advantage of EKS Pod Identity over IRSA, as shown in this batch''s content?', '["Pod Identity works on Kubernetes versions older than 1.24, unlike IRSA", "Pod Identity does not require setting up an OIDC provider for the cluster -- it associates an IAM role with a ServiceAccount more directly via aws eks create-pod-identity-association", "Pod Identity grants broader permissions automatically by default", "Pod Identity replaces the need for IAM roles entirely"]', 1, 'IRSA depends on configuring an OIDC identity provider and crafting a trust policy referencing it. EKS Pod Identity simplifies this by removing the OIDC provider setup step, associating roles with ServiceAccounts through a dedicated EKS API instead.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'intermediate', 'In the EBS-backed StorageClass example, why is volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer used instead of the default Immediate?', '["WaitForFirstConsumer disables dynamic provisioning entirely", "It delays volume creation until a pod actually claims the PVC and is scheduled, so the volume is created in the same Availability Zone as the node the pod lands on -- EBS volumes cannot attach across AZs", "It is required for VolumeSnapshot support", "It only affects performance, not correctness"]', 1, 'EBS volumes are zonal -- a volume created in one AZ cannot attach to a node in another. WaitForFirstConsumer defers provisioning until the scheduler has picked a node, ensuring the volume is created in a compatible AZ, avoiding a class of scheduling failures Immediate binding can cause.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'intermediate', 'What does the disruption.consolidationPolicy: WhenUnderutilized setting on a Karpenter NodePool do?', '["It immediately terminates any node with less than 100% CPU usage", "It allows Karpenter to consolidate workloads and remove underutilized nodes to reduce cost, rather than leaving partially-empty nodes running indefinitely", "It disables all node termination for that pool", "It only applies to Spot instances, never On-Demand"]', 1, 'Consolidation is Karpenter''s cost-optimization mechanism: it periodically evaluates whether pods could be repacked onto fewer/cheaper nodes and, if so, drains and removes the now-unnecessary node -- the opposite of a fixed-size node group that stays provisioned regardless of actual utilization.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/aws-eks/advanced ----------
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'advanced', 'Why does the node-termination-handler config enable both enableSpotInterruptionDraining and enableScheduledEventDraining?', '["They are redundant options and only one is actually needed", "They handle two different disruption sources -- Spot interruption notices (2-minute warning before reclaim) and scheduled EC2 maintenance events -- both of which require gracefully draining pods off the node before it disappears", "enableScheduledEventDraining only applies to On-Demand instances, unrelated to Spot", "Both settings only affect logging, not actual node behavior"]', 1, 'Spot interruptions and scheduled maintenance events are distinct AWS-initiated disruption signals with different triggers; enabling both drain paths ensures pods are gracefully evicted (rather than abruptly killed) regardless of which kind of disruption is about to happen.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'advanced', 'What does Goldilocks actually do, based on the workflow shown (label a namespace, wait 24h, view recommendations)?', '["It automatically resizes running pods in real time without any review", "It observes actual resource usage over time (via Vertical Pod Autoscaler in recommendation mode) and surfaces suggested CPU/memory requests and limits for review, rather than applying changes automatically", "It replaces the need for a StorageClass", "It only works for Fargate-backed pods"]', 1, 'Goldilocks builds on VPA''s recommendation mode to show right-sizing suggestions based on observed usage -- a human still reviews and applies (or ignores) the recommendations rather than the tool silently resizing workloads.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'advanced', 'In the aws eks create-access-entry example granting DevOpsRole the system:masters Kubernetes group, what capability does this represent within EKS''s newer access model?', '["A way to grant AWS IAM permissions to Kubernetes pods", "EKS access entries let IAM principals be mapped directly to Kubernetes RBAC groups through an EKS-managed API, as an alternative/successor to manually editing the aws-auth ConfigMap", "A mechanism exclusively for Fargate profile selection", "A method for rotating the cluster''s TLS certificates"]', 1, 'Access entries are EKS''s API-driven way of mapping IAM principals to Kubernetes RBAC identities (like the built-in system:masters group), reducing reliance on directly editing the aws-auth ConfigMap, which was historically the only way to grant cluster access to an IAM principal.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'advanced', 'Why would a team registering multiple EKS clusters with a central ArgoCD instance store each cluster''s kubeconfig via an ExternalSecret rather than as a plain Kubernetes Secret checked into a manifest?', '["ExternalSecret is required by ArgoCD and has no security benefit", "It keeps the actual cluster credentials in a secrets backend (like AWS Secrets Manager) rather than in a Kubernetes manifest or Git repo, where they could otherwise leak via repo access or manifest review", "Plain Kubernetes Secrets cannot store kubeconfig data at all", "ExternalSecret only works with GitOps tools other than ArgoCD"]', 1, 'A raw Secret manifest committed to Git (even encrypted at rest in etcd) risks broader exposure than necessary. ExternalSecret syncs the actual sensitive value from a dedicated secrets backend at runtime, so the credential itself never has to live in a manifest reviewed or stored in Git.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'aws-eks', 'advanced', 'What does aws eks update-nodegroup-version actually do to the nodes in a managed node group?', '["It patches the running kernel on each node in place without any downtime", "It performs a rolling replacement of nodes in the group with new instances running the updated AMI/Kubernetes version, respecting configured max-unavailable settings", "It only updates the node group''s IAM role, not the nodes themselves", "It deletes all pods in the cluster before proceeding"]', 1, 'Managed node group upgrades work by rolling out new nodes (on the target version/AMI) and cordoning/draining old ones in a controlled sequence, rather than modifying existing instances in place -- consistent with how immutable infrastructure upgrades are generally handled.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/cloudformation/overview ----------
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'overview', 'How does CloudFormation determine the order in which to create resources in a template?', '["Strictly top-to-bottom, in the order resources appear in the file", "It infers dependency order automatically from intrinsic function references like !Ref and !GetAtt between resources (and explicit DependsOn where needed) -- a resource is created only after everything it references exists", "Alphabetically by logical resource name", "Resources are always created in parallel with no ordering"]', 1, 'CloudFormation builds a dependency graph from !Ref/!GetAtt usage (and any explicit DependsOn) and creates resources in the order that graph requires -- e.g. a Subnet referencing a VPC via !Ref VPC is guaranteed to be created after that VPC.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'overview', 'In the corrected VPC template, why did the NatGateway resource fail to be self-contained until an ElasticIP resource was added?', '["NatGateway does not require an Elastic IP at all", "NatGateway''s AllocationId property referenced !GetAtt ElasticIP.AllocationId, but no ElasticIP (AWS::EC2::EIP) resource existed in the template to provide that attribute -- a NAT Gateway does not allocate its own EIP automatically within a template", "AllocationId is optional and CloudFormation would have used a default IP", "ElasticIP resources are created automatically whenever a VPC exists"]', 1, 'A reference to a resource''s attribute (via !GetAtt) requires that resource to actually be declared in the template. The original template referenced ElasticIP.AllocationId without ever declaring an AWS::EC2::EIP resource -- a real bug that would fail at deploy time.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'overview', 'What is the practical difference between aws cloudformation validate-template and actually running create-stack?', '["They are functionally identical", "validate-template only checks template syntax and structure (JSON/YAML validity, intrinsic function usage) -- it does not check whether referenced resources actually exist or whether the account has permission to create them; only create-stack (or a change set) attempts the real deployment", "validate-template actually creates the resources in a sandbox account first", "create-stack only works after a successful validate-template call"]', 1, 'validate-template is a safe, side-effect-free syntax check. It will not catch, for example, a resource block that''s missing a required property''s dependency at a semantic level the way an actual stack operation (or a Change Set, which performs real planning) would.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'overview', 'Why does the template use Fn::FindInMap with the pseudo-parameter !Ref AWS::Region rather than hardcoding one AMI ID?', '["FindInMap is required syntax and has no functional purpose here", "AMI IDs are region-specific -- the same AMI does not exist under the same ID in every region, so the Mappings section provides a per-region lookup that resolves correctly regardless of which region the stack is deployed into", "It makes the template deploy faster", "It is only needed when using nested stacks"]', 1, 'An AMI is tied to a specific region. Hardcoding a single AMI ID would break the template in any region other than the one it was copied from -- the RegionAMI mapping combined with !Ref AWS::Region lets the same template resolve the correct AMI per region automatically.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'overview', 'Why does this batch flag hardcoded AMI IDs in example templates as needing verification, even though the template structure itself is correct?', '["AMI IDs never change once published by AWS", "AMI IDs are specific artifacts that AWS republishes over time (patched builds, new base images) -- a hardcoded ID can become stale/deregistered, so real templates should look up the current AMI (e.g. via the public SSM parameter) rather than hardcoding one", "Hardcoded AMI IDs are a security vulnerability by themselves", "This only matters for Windows AMIs, not Linux"]', 1, 'This is a version-specific detail under the accuracy-confidence rule: the TEMPLATE MECHANICS (Mappings, FindInMap) are foundational and correct, but the specific AMI ID values are exactly the kind of thing that goes stale and should be verified/looked up dynamically rather than trusted as permanently valid.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/cloudformation/fundamentals ----------
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'fundamentals', 'What does DeletionPolicy: Snapshot on the Database resource actually change about stack deletion behavior?', '["It prevents the stack from ever being deleted", "Before the RDS instance is deleted as part of a stack delete/replace, CloudFormation takes a final snapshot of it, rather than deleting the data with no backup", "It skips deleting the resource entirely, identical to DeletionPolicy: Retain", "It only applies to resources without a Condition attached"]', 1, 'DeletionPolicy: Snapshot specifically applies to resource types that support snapshots (like RDS, EBS, ElastiCache/Redshift) -- it triggers a final snapshot as part of the delete rather than simply retaining the live resource (Retain) or deleting it outright with no backup (Delete, the default).', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'fundamentals', 'Why did this batch fix "aws cloudformation describe-stack-outputs" to instead use describe-stacks with a --query filter?', '["describe-stack-outputs is simply a slower version of the same command", "describe-stack-outputs is not a real AWS CLI subcommand -- Outputs is a field returned by describe-stacks, not a separate API call, so the corrected command extracts it via --query instead", "Both commands exist but only one supports YAML templates", "describe-stack-outputs was deprecated in a recent CLI version"]', 1, 'This was a genuine hallucinated command in the original content -- the AWS CLI has no describe-stack-outputs subcommand. Stack outputs are simply a field (Outputs) inside the response from the real describe-stacks call.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'fundamentals', 'What is the practical purpose of the {{resolve:secretsmanager:...}} dynamic reference used for MasterUserPassword in the RDS example?', '["It generates a new random password every time the stack is updated", "It lets the template pull the actual secret value from Secrets Manager at deploy/update time, so the real password never has to appear as plaintext inside the template file itself", "It is purely cosmetic and has no effect on the deployed resource", "It only works for parameters, not resource properties"]', 1, 'Dynamic references like {{resolve:secretsmanager:...}} are resolved by CloudFormation at deploy time by fetching the current value from Secrets Manager -- the template file itself never contains the actual secret, which is the entire point of using it instead of a plaintext parameter.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'fundamentals', 'In a Change Set workflow (create-change-set, describe-change-set, execute-change-set), what is the purpose of the describe step specifically?', '["It is optional and only exists for logging purposes", "It lets you review exactly what CloudFormation is about to change (Add/Modify/Remove, and whether a Modify requires resource replacement) BEFORE committing to execute-change-set, avoiding surprise replacements or deletions in production", "It automatically applies the safest half of the proposed changes", "It rolls back any changes made since the last successful stack update"]', 1, 'This mirrors "terraform plan" -- describe-change-set surfaces the concrete list of pending changes, including which ones require replacement (destroy + recreate) versus in-place update, so a human can catch a dangerous change before execute-change-set actually applies it.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'fundamentals', 'Does changing the InstanceType property on an already-deployed AWS::EC2::Instance typically require CloudFormation to replace the resource?', '["Yes, any property change on any resource always forces replacement", "No -- InstanceType is generally an in-place-updatable property for EC2 instances, unlike some properties (e.g. a VPC''s CIDR block) that do force replacement when changed", "It depends entirely on which AWS region the stack is in", "CloudFormation cannot update EC2 instance properties at all, only create/delete them"]', 1, 'Whether a property change causes an in-place update or a full replacement is defined per-property in the resource type''s specification. InstanceType on AWS::EC2::Instance is a commonly-cited example of an in-place-updatable property, unlike identity-defining properties on other resource types.', 'advanced'),

-- ---------- cloud/cloudformation/intermediate ----------
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'intermediate', 'What does !ImportValue prod-network-VpcId require to have already happened in a different stack?', '["Nothing -- ImportValue works across any two stacks with no prior setup","That other stack must have declared an Outputs section with an Export whose Name resolves to exactly \"prod-network-VpcId\" -- ImportValue can only reference values another stack has explicitly exported","The importing stack must be a nested stack of the exporting one","ImportValue only works within the same AWS account, never cross-account"]', 1, 'Cross-stack references require the source stack to explicitly Export a named value in its Outputs section; ImportValue in another stack references that value by the exact export Name -- there is no way to reach into another stack''s resources without an explicit Export/Import pair.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'intermediate', 'Why can you not simply delete a stack whose resources are still referenced by another stack via ImportValue?', '["CloudFormation has no such restriction -- any stack can always be deleted", "CloudFormation blocks deletion (or removal of the exported value) of a stack whose Output is still actively imported elsewhere, to prevent breaking the importing stack''s resources that depend on that value", "Only nested stacks have this restriction, not sibling stacks", "This restriction only applies to VPC-related exports"]', 1, 'This is a deliberate safety mechanism: if Stack A exports a VPC ID that Stack B has imported, CloudFormation will not let you delete that export (or the stack) while Stack B''s dependency is still active, preventing a dangling reference.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'intermediate', 'What is the purpose of the AWS::CloudFormation::CustomResource type combined with a Lambda-backed ServiceToken?', '["It lets a template create only native AWS resource types, nothing else", "It lets a stack invoke custom logic (via Lambda) during create/update/delete, useful for provisioning things CloudFormation has no native resource type for -- like seeding a database with initial data", "CustomResource is only usable inside nested stacks", "It replaces the need for IAM roles on other resources in the stack"]', 1, 'Custom Resources are CloudFormation''s extension mechanism: on create/update/delete, CloudFormation invokes the Lambda function named in ServiceToken and waits for a cfnresponse signal, letting the stack incorporate logic (like seeding a database) that has no native CloudFormation resource type.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'intermediate', 'In a nested stack setup, why does the AppStack resource use DependsOn: NetworkStack in addition to referencing NetworkStack.Outputs.VpcId via !GetAtt?', '["DependsOn is redundant here since !GetAtt already implies ordering in every case", "It is shown for clarity even though the !GetAtt reference to NetworkStack''s output already implies the dependency -- but explicit DependsOn is sometimes still needed when a dependency isn''t expressed through any direct attribute reference", "DependsOn is required syntax on every AWS::CloudFormation::Stack resource", "It disables rollback for the AppStack specifically"]', 1, 'When a resource references another via !Ref/!GetAtt, CloudFormation already infers the dependency; DependsOn becomes necessary specifically for dependencies that aren''t expressed through any such reference. In this example the GetAtt reference already establishes ordering, and the explicit DependsOn reinforces the same intent.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'intermediate', 'Why must a nested stack''s TemplateURL point to an S3 location rather than a local file path?', '["CloudFormation nested stacks do not support S3 at all -- only local files", "CloudFormation''s API operates server-side against AWS -- it has no access to a local filesystem, so the child template must already be uploaded somewhere AWS can fetch it from, typically S3", "S3 is required only for templates larger than 1MB", "TemplateURL is optional and defaults to the parent template''s own body"]', 1, 'A nested stack''s child template is fetched by the CloudFormation service itself at deploy time, not by whatever machine is running the deploy command -- so it needs a URL the AWS service can reach (S3 is the standard choice), not a path on the local machine.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/cloudformation/advanced ----------
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'advanced', 'What does --permission-model SERVICE_MANAGED enable when creating a StackSet?', '["It requires manually creating an IAM role in every target account before deployment", "It lets CloudFormation StackSets use AWS Organizations to automatically deploy to accounts within a specified OU, without needing manually pre-created execution roles in every target account", "It restricts the StackSet to a single account only", "It is required for every StackSet regardless of target accounts"]', 1, 'SERVICE_MANAGED permission model integrates with AWS Organizations, letting StackSets deploy across an OU''s accounts (and even auto-deploy to new accounts that join it) without the manual per-account IAM role setup that the alternative SELF_MANAGED model requires.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'advanced', 'What does drift detection identify?', '["Resources that were created by CloudFormation but have since had their actual configuration changed outside CloudFormation (e.g. via console or another tool), so the real state no longer matches the template", "Whether a stack''s deployment took longer than expected", "Cost differences between the planned and actual stack", "Whether a template file has valid YAML syntax"]', 0, 'Drift detection compares a stack''s resources'' actual current configuration in AWS against what the template originally specified -- it surfaces cases where someone made a manual change (e.g. via the console) that CloudFormation is now unaware of.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'advanced', 'Why does this batch''s corrected DNSSEC example create and activate the key-signing key BEFORE calling enable-hosted-zone-dnssec, reversing the original order?', '["The order does not actually matter for this workflow", "A key-signing key must exist and be ACTIVE before DNSSEC signing can be turned on for the zone -- enabling signing first, with no active KSK, does not correctly complete the setup", "enable-hosted-zone-dnssec automatically creates its own KSK if none exists, making order irrelevant", "This only matters when using a CMK from a Region other than the hosted zone''s"]', 1, 'DNSSEC signing depends on having an active key-signing key to actually sign the zone with -- the corrected order (create KSK, activate it, then enable signing) reflects that dependency; the original content had the steps in an order that wouldn''t actually work as a sequence.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'advanced', 'What does a Stack Policy with Effect: Deny on Action: Update:Delete actually protect against?', '["It prevents the stack from ever being deleted under any circumstances", "It prevents a specific resource (or resources matching the policy''s Resource selector) from being deleted as a side effect of a stack UPDATE (e.g. a template change that would replace/remove it) -- delete-stack itself is a separate operation not governed by this policy", "It blocks all IAM users from making any changes to the account", "It only applies during stack creation, not updates"]', 1, 'Stack policies specifically govern what update operations are allowed to do to specific resources during a stack UPDATE -- they are a safeguard against a template change accidentally deleting/replacing a critical resource, not a blanket protection against explicit stack deletion via delete-stack.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'cloudformation', 'advanced', 'What is the AWS::Include transform used for, as distinct from the AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31 (SAM) transform?', '["Both transforms are functionally identical", "AWS::Include embeds reusable template snippets from S3 directly into the template at deploy time (e.g. shared tagging blocks); AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31 expands SAM shorthand resource types (like AWS::Serverless::Function) into their full underlying CloudFormation resource definitions", "AWS::Include is only usable inside nested stacks", "AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31 replaces the need for any Resources section"]', 1, 'These solve different problems: AWS::Include is a generic snippet-inclusion mechanism for reusing template fragments, while the SAM transform is a macro that expands higher-level serverless-specific shorthand syntax into the equivalent full CloudFormation resources (Lambda function + IAM role + API Gateway, etc.) before deployment.', 'advanced'),

-- ---------- cloud/route53/overview ----------
('cloud', 'route53', 'overview', 'Why can a CNAME record not be used at a domain''s zone apex (e.g. example.com, not www.example.com)?', '["AWS-specific restriction unrelated to the DNS standard", "The DNS standard prohibits a CNAME from coexisting with other record types (like the required NS and SOA records) at the same name, and the zone apex must have those records -- Route 53''s ALIAS record type exists specifically to work around this at the apex", "CNAME records are deprecated and no longer supported anywhere", "It is only a limitation for private hosted zones, not public ones"]', 1, 'This is a DNS-standard-level rule, not an AWS-specific one: a name with a CNAME cannot have any other record type at that same name, but the apex of a zone must have NS and SOA records -- which is exactly the constraint Route 53''s non-standard ALIAS record type was invented to route around.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'overview', 'In weighted routing, what does setting one record''s Weight to 90 and another''s to 10 (for the same name) actually control?', '["Exactly 90% and 10% of DNS QUERIES receive each answer with mathematical precision every time", "The proportional likelihood Route 53 returns each record when answering a query for that name -- used for gradually shifting traffic (e.g. blue/green, canary) rather than an instant all-or-nothing cutover", "Which record is used for a health check, not actual traffic distribution", "The order in which the two records are checked for expiration"]', 1, 'Weighted routing distributes the probability of which record gets returned per query, roughly proportional to the weights -- it is the mechanism behind gradual traffic shifting (e.g. canary releases) rather than a strict, per-request guaranteed percentage.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'overview', 'What is the functional difference between Latency-based routing and Geolocation routing in Route 53?', '["They are two names for the exact same feature", "Latency routing sends users to the region with the lowest MEASURED latency (based on AWS''s own latency data between locations and regions); Geolocation routes based on the user''s actual geographic location, which the operator explicitly assigns per record, regardless of measured latency", "Geolocation only works for US-based users", "Latency routing requires a health check; Geolocation does not"]', 1, 'Latency routing is driven by AWS''s continuously measured network latency data -- it optimizes for speed. Geolocation is driven by a deliberate business/compliance decision (e.g. route EU users to an EU endpoint for GDPR reasons) regardless of which endpoint would actually be fastest.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'overview', 'What happens to a weighted or failover record if its associated Route 53 health check reports the endpoint as unhealthy?', '["Nothing changes -- health checks are informational only unless manually reviewed", "Route 53 automatically stops returning that record in responses (removes it from rotation / triggers failover to the alternate), without any manual intervention required", "The record is permanently deleted from the hosted zone", "DNS queries for that name start returning NXDOMAIN errors"]', 1, 'This is the core value of attaching health checks to routing policies: Route 53 actively excludes unhealthy targets from what it returns (in weighted rotation) or fails over to the secondary (in failover routing) automatically, based on health check state.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'overview', 'Why does a low TTL (e.g. 60 seconds) matter specifically before a planned DNS cutover, according to this batch''s content?', '["Low TTL makes DNS queries resolve faster in general, unrelated to cutovers", "A record''s TTL controls how long resolvers are allowed to cache the old answer -- lowering it before a change shrinks the worst-case window during which some clients could still be served the stale/old value after the record is updated", "TTL only affects Route 53''s own internal caching, not client-side resolver caching", "Lowering TTL is required by AWS before any record can be changed at all"]', 1, 'TTL governs caching by recursive resolvers along the resolution path, not Route 53 itself. A lower TTL set in advance of a cutover means any resolver that already cached the old value will re-check sooner, shrinking the window where different users could see inconsistent answers during the transition.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/route53/fundamentals ----------
('cloud', 'route53', 'fundamentals', 'Why does the ALIAS record example for an ALB omit a TTL value, while the plain A record example includes one?', '["Omitting TTL is a mistake in the example and should always be set explicitly", "ALIAS records are an AWS-managed extension -- Route 53 automatically tracks and resolves to the target resource''s current IP without a TTL you configure, unlike a standard A record where you set the caching duration explicitly", "TTL is only required for records pointing to non-AWS resources", "ALIAS records never expire and are cached forever by resolvers"]', 1, 'Because ALIAS is a Route 53-specific mechanism (not a real DNS record type at the protocol level), AWS manages resolving it to the target''s current value dynamically -- there is no user-configurable TTL the way there is for a standard A/CNAME record.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'fundamentals', 'In the weighted routing Terraform example, why does each aws_route53_record resource need a distinct set_identifier even though they share the same name and type?', '["set_identifier is purely cosmetic and has no functional purpose", "Multiple records with the same name/type but different routing policies (weighted, failover, etc.) require a unique set_identifier to distinguish which record is which within that record set -- without it, Route 53 cannot tell the resource sets apart", "set_identifier only matters for CNAME records, not A records", "It sets the DNS priority, like an MX record''s priority field"]', 1, 'Weighted (and other multi-record routing policy types) require a unique set_identifier per record sharing the same name/type, since there are now multiple resource record sets for that exact name and Route 53 needs a way to distinguish and manage each one individually.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'fundamentals', 'What does dig +trace actually show that a plain dig query does not?', '["The exact physical server location of the domain owner", "The full resolution chain, starting from the root name servers and walking down through each delegation (TLD servers, then the domain''s authoritative name servers) to the final answer", "Only the DNS record''s TTL value, nothing else", "Whether the domain uses DNSSEC, and nothing about the actual record chain"]', 1, 'A plain dig query just returns the final answer from whichever resolver you queried. dig +trace instead walks the entire delegation chain manually, starting at the root servers, showing each step of how authority for the name gets delegated down to the domain''s own authoritative servers.', 'beginner'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'fundamentals', 'What is the purpose of the "UPSERT" action (as opposed to "CREATE") in a Route 53 change-resource-record-sets call?', '["UPSERT deletes the record instead of creating or updating it", "UPSERT creates the record if it does not exist yet, or updates it in place if it already does -- avoiding the need to check existence first and choose CREATE vs an update action explicitly", "UPSERT only works for ALIAS records, not standard A/CNAME records", "UPSERT is required for every Route 53 API call regardless of intent"]', 1, 'UPSERT is a convenience action that handles both the create and update cases in one call, which is why it is the most commonly used action for change batches -- CREATE would instead fail if the record already existed.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'fundamentals', 'Why does a private hosted zone need to be explicitly associated with a VPC (via the --vpc parameter or aws_route53_zone''s vpc block) rather than being reachable automatically?', '["Private hosted zones are reachable from any VPC in the account by default", "A private hosted zone only resolves for requests originating inside VPCs it has been explicitly associated with -- this scoping is what keeps the internal DNS names from being resolvable from the public internet or unrelated VPCs", "The association only affects billing, not actual resolution", "Private hosted zones require a public hosted zone with the same name to exist first"]', 1, 'The explicit VPC association is the mechanism that scopes a private hosted zone''s resolution to only the VPC(s) actually attached to it -- without that association, DNS resolvers inside a VPC would have no way to know the private zone exists at all, keeping the internal names genuinely private.', 'intermediate'),

-- ---------- cloud/route53/intermediate ----------
('cloud', 'route53', 'intermediate', 'In the weighted-routing change-batch example, why does each ResourceRecordSet need both a SetIdentifier and a Weight to work correctly?', '["Only Weight is required; SetIdentifier is optional metadata", "SetIdentifier distinguishes the two records sharing the same Name/Type from each other, while Weight determines the proportional traffic split between them -- both are required together for weighted routing to function", "SetIdentifier sets the DNS TTL for that specific record", "Weight is ignored unless a health check is also attached"]', 1, 'Weighted routing needs multiple ResourceRecordSets for the same name distinguished by SetIdentifier, each carrying its own Weight -- both fields work together; SetIdentifier makes the records distinct entries, Weight determines their relative traffic share.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'intermediate', 'Why did this batch fix the JSON change-batch string for failover routing so the health check ID uses ''"$HEALTH_CHECK_ID"'' instead of "$HEALTH_CHECK_ID" inside single quotes?', '["The original syntax was valid JSON and needed no change","Inside a fully single-quoted bash string, shell variables like $HEALTH_CHECK_ID are NOT expanded -- the literal text \"$HEALTH_CHECK_ID\" would be sent to AWS instead of its actual value; closing and reopening the quote lets bash substitute the variable before assembling the string","Double-quoting variables inside JSON is never valid regardless of context","HealthCheckId does not accept variable substitution in the Route 53 API"]', 1, 'This was a real shell-quoting bug: single quotes in bash prevent ALL variable expansion inside them, including $HEALTH_CHECK_ID. The fix closes the single-quoted string, inserts a double-quoted (expanding) reference to the variable, then reopens the single-quoted string -- a standard bash technique for mixing literal text and variable expansion.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'intermediate', 'What does associating an additional VPC with an existing private hosted zone (via associate-vpc-with-hosted-zone) enable?', '["It makes the private zone publicly resolvable from the internet", "It extends the same private hosted zone''s resolution to a second VPC, so both VPCs can resolve the same internal names (e.g. for cross-VPC service discovery) without duplicating the zone", "It merges the two VPCs into a single network", "It is required before any record can be added to the private zone at all"]', 1, 'A private hosted zone can be associated with multiple VPCs (including across accounts, with the right permissions) -- this lets several VPCs share one consistent set of internal DNS names rather than each needing its own separate private zone.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'intermediate', 'For a CloudWatch alarm monitoring the Route 53 HealthCheckStatus metric, why does the example use ComparisonOperator: LessThanThreshold with Threshold: 1?', '["HealthCheckStatus reports a percentage, and the alarm fires below 1%", "HealthCheckStatus reports 1 for healthy and 0 for unhealthy, so alarming when the value drops below 1 (i.e. equals 0) is how the alarm detects the unhealthy state", "The threshold value is arbitrary and has no relationship to the metric''s actual meaning", "LessThanThreshold is required syntax and does not reflect the metric''s real values"]', 1, 'Route 53''s HealthCheckStatus metric reports a binary-like value (1 = healthy, 0 = unhealthy). Alarming on "less than 1" is effectively alarming on "equals 0," i.e. the unhealthy state -- a common pattern for binary health metrics that don''t have a dedicated "equals" comparison operator in CloudWatch.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'intermediate', 'What is the practical use case for a Calculated health check, as opposed to a plain Endpoint health check?', '["Calculated health checks monitor CloudWatch metrics instead of an HTTP endpoint","Calculated health checks combine the results of multiple other health checks using AND/OR logic, useful for representing composite health (e.g. \"healthy only if at least 2 of 3 backend health checks pass\") rather than a single endpoint''s status","Calculated health checks are purely a billing category with no functional difference","Only Calculated health checks can be attached to failover routing records"]', 1, 'A Calculated health check does not probe an endpoint itself -- it evaluates the combined results of other health checks (via AND/OR child-health-check logic), which is useful for expressing composite availability logic across multiple underlying checks.', 'advanced'),

-- ---------- cloud/route53/advanced ----------
('cloud', 'route53', 'advanced', 'What must happen at a domain''s registrar (the parent zone) after enabling DNSSEC signing on a Route 53 hosted zone, for the chain of trust to actually be complete?', '["Nothing further is required -- enabling signing in Route 53 is sufficient on its own", "A DS (Delegation Signer) record must be added at the registrar/parent zone, linking the parent zone''s trust to this zone''s key-signing key -- without it, DNSSEC validation for this zone has no chain of trust from the parent", "The registrar automatically detects and adds the DS record with no action needed", "DS records are only required for private hosted zones, not public ones"]', 1, 'DNSSEC''s chain of trust works by each parent zone vouching for its child zone via a DS record pointing at the child''s key-signing key. Signing the zone in Route 53 alone does not establish that link -- the DS record has to be published at the parent/registrar level to complete the chain.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'advanced', 'What is the functional difference between a Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoint and an outbound endpoint?', '["They are interchangeable and both allow traffic in either direction", "An inbound endpoint lets on-premises/other networks query Route 53 (including private hosted zones) FROM outside AWS; an outbound endpoint lets Route 53 Resolver forward queries FROM the VPC OUT to an on-premises or other external DNS server", "Both endpoints only work for public hosted zones, never private ones", "Inbound endpoints are only used for health checks, not actual DNS queries"]', 1, 'These are two different directions of a hybrid-DNS setup: inbound endpoints accept DNS queries coming IN from outside AWS (e.g. an on-prem network resolving a private hosted zone name), while outbound endpoints forward queries going OUT from the VPC to an external DNS server (e.g. resolving an on-prem-only domain).', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'advanced', 'In Geoproximity routing, what does a positive Bias value do to a region''s effective coverage area?', '["It has no effect on coverage -- Bias only affects display order in the console", "A positive bias expands the geographic area from which traffic is routed to that region, effectively pulling in users who would otherwise be closer to a different region", "A positive bias always routes 100% of traffic to that region regardless of distance", "Bias only applies to Geolocation routing, not Geoproximity"]', 1, 'Geoproximity routing bases routing on physical distance by default, but a positive Bias value shifts the boundary of a region''s effective catchment area outward -- pulling in some traffic that would otherwise be geographically closer to a neighboring region, useful for deliberately shifting load.', 'advanced'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'advanced', 'Why is Multivalue Answer routing explicitly described as NOT a replacement for a real load balancer like an ALB?', '["Multivalue routing cannot return more than one IP address at all", "DNS-level multivalue routing only performs basic, client-side selection among up to 8 healthy IPs with health-check-based exclusion -- it lacks the connection-level features (SSL termination, path-based routing, connection draining, weighted target groups) that an actual load balancer provides", "Multivalue routing requires a Network Load Balancer to function at all", "It is a deprecated feature that AWS recommends against using"]', 1, 'Multivalue Answer routing gives DNS clients a choice among several healthy IPs (basic load distribution and failover at the DNS resolution level), but it has none of an actual load balancer''s Layer 4/7 capabilities -- it operates purely at the level of "which IP does the DNS answer contain," not real traffic management.', 'intermediate'),
('cloud', 'route53', 'advanced', 'Why does split-view (private + public) DNS for the same domain name require two separate hosted zones rather than one?', '["Route 53 does not actually support split-view DNS for the same domain name", "A hosted zone is either public (resolvable from the internet) or private (resolvable only from associated VPCs) -- to have the SAME domain name resolve differently depending on whether the query comes from inside or outside the VPC, you need one zone of each type, both named identically", "Private and public zones for the same name conflict and Route 53 rejects the second one", "Split-view DNS requires a third-party DNS provider in addition to Route 53"]', 1, 'Since a hosted zone''s visibility (public vs private) is a property of the zone itself, achieving different answers for internal vs external queries against the SAME domain name requires maintaining two zones with that identical name -- one public, one private -- each with its own independent record set.', 'advanced')

ON CONFLICT (academy_slug, technology_slug, section_slug, question) DO NOTHING;
